Software that is not free software

Not everything in an Amethystora image is free software. The parts below are
redistributed as their makers' licences allow, each under its own terms and not
under the Apache License; each licence ships with its package in
usr/share/licenses:

  - DisplayLink Manager (package displaylink), which drives DisplayLink docks
    and adapters, under the DisplayLink Software License Agreement in
    usr/share/licenses/displaylink. It is licensed for use only with products
    that incorporate DisplayLink technology.
  - Device firmware and processor microcode (linux-firmware, the *-firmware
    packages and microcode_ctl), redistributable but not modifiable.
  - On the nvidia-open and nvidia-lts images, the user-space part of the NVIDIA
    driver, and on the nvidia-lts images its kernel module too, under NVIDIA's
    software licence.

Microsoft's firmware for its wireless adapter for Xbox controllers is not in
any image: no licence lets it be passed on. `ame apps xbox-adapter on`
downloads it from Microsoft on the machine that asks, under Microsoft's terms
of use. For the same reason the source package of xone-kmod-common, the
adapter driver's common package, which holds Microsoft's Windows driver
packages, is not carried in usr/src/amethystora; its own files are a modprobe
line, a README and the GPL, at https://github.com/ublue-os/packages
(akmods/xone) and https://github.com/OpenGamingCollective/xonedo.

Source code

Amethystora images contain software under licences that require its source
code to be made available to those who receive it: the GNU General Public
License and the GNU Lesser General Public License, the Mozilla Public License,
the Eclipse Public License, the Common Development and Distribution License,
and the licence of the Fraunhofer FDK AAC codec, among others. The file
SOURCES, beside this one in usr/share/licenses/amethystora of every image,
lists each package in that image with its licence, the source package it was
built from, and where that source package is:

  - what Fedora built, in Fedora's build system, Koji, at the address given;
  - what others built (RPM Fusion, negativo17, Fedora Copr) under one of those
    licences, in the image itself, in usr/src/amethystora. Those makers
    publish only their current build of each package, so the source package
    an image was built from is fetched as the image is built and travels
    with it;
  - the rest, from the maker and at the address named there.

Everything not installed from a package is built from
https://github.com/iarsslen/amethystora at the commit the image names as
BUILD_ID in /usr/lib/os-release; its build_files pin each upstream release
they fetch.

An installer ISO carries one image and the installer that puts it on a disk.
The installer is a small system of its own, made of Fedora's packages as
Fedora built them, with generic-logos and generic-release in place of Fedora's
logo and release packages. The file INSTALLER-PACKAGES at the top of the ISO,
beside a copy of this one, lists them; Fedora publishes the source of each in
Koji.

Three of those packages are changed on the ISOs, so that the installer neither
names Fedora nor sends its crash reports to Fedora's Bugzilla. On the live ISO,
iso/live/build.sh changes Anaconda (GPL-2.0-or-later,
https://github.com/rhinstaller/anaconda): it writes Amethystora for Fedora in
its welcome window and its launcher, and has liveinst call the release final;
and it hides the feedback section of anaconda-webui (LGPL-2.1-or-later and
MIT, https://github.com/rhinstaller/anaconda-webui) with one line added to its
stylesheet. On both ISOs, the files in iso/product/etc/libreport take the place
of libreport-anaconda's (GPL-2.0-or-later, https://github.com/abrt/libreport)
rules for installer crashes. Each change is made from the repository when an
ISO is built, and the files that change say so.

